# =============================================================================
# prepare_data.R
# Workshop 7: exporting, opening, checking, cleaning, and merging iMotions Lab 11.1 data
# Martin's Family Clothing Consumer Behavior Research Laboratory, JSU
#
# SIMULATED DATA. Every file this script reads was generated by make_simulated_data.py (the seed is
# printed in the first line of every export). No person was recorded. The files imitate the export
# bundle of one screen based study, "SIMULATED Multimodal Ad Study": 24 participants (P01 to P24) at
# the lab desktop with the Smart Eye Aurora (60 Hz), the respondent camera with Affectiva AFFDEX, and
# a Shimmer3 GSR+ (128 Hz). Slides: Instructions, Baseline (60 s), Ad_A and Ad_B (30 s video ads,
# order counterbalanced: AB or BA), Questionnaire. AOIs on each ad: Logo, Product, Headline. One
# stimulus annotation per ad, "EndCard" (25 to 30 s after the ad starts).
#
# What this script does: it turns eight export families with different layouts, different key
# names, and real world problems into two analysis ready tables, with a codebook, a cleaning log,
# and a quality assurance (QA) report. Every decision is printed and logged, so that another person
# can repeat it (Wilson et al., 2017; Broman & Woo, 2018).
#
# The exclusion rule, written in the study protocol before collection (2026-11-09):
#   R1. Use only the recordings that the session log marks "Use recording = Yes".
#   R2. Exclude a participant from ALL analyses when, in the data quality file (_DataQuality.txt),
#       Eye tracking_Quality is below 90 percent on Ad_A or Ad_B (lab manual Appendix D: more than
#       90 percent valid gaze samples), or Affectiva AFFDEX_Quality is below 80 percent on Ad_A or
#       Ad_B (the lab's 80 percent face rule, Workshop 3).
#   R3. For GSR analyses ONLY, treat a stimulus as missing when its Signal Duration (ms) is below
#       80 percent of its Duration (ms) in the GSR Peak Metrics file. The participant stays in every
#       other analysis.
#
# What the script does, in order:
#   STEP 0.  Packages, paths, and helper functions (reading, keys, the cleaning log)
#   STEP 1.  Inventory of the export bundle (files, sizes, separators, header rows)
#   STEP 2.  The session log: the roster and the recording to participant crosswalk
#   STEP 3.  Read the R metrics exports (GSR, FEA, AOI): skip the # block, find #DATA
#   STEP 4.  Read the survey exports and check the student's Excel copy
#   STEP 5.  Read the data quality file and the annotation exports
#   STEP 6.  Audit the raw respondent keys in every file
#   STEP 7.  Standardize the keys, map recordings to participants, drop the aborted recording
#   STEP 8.  Standardize the stimulus keys and keep the analysis stimuli
#   STEP 9.  Respondent details: Gender, Age, Group, and the order of the ads
#   STEP 10. Survey: from LABELID_ and LABELVALUE_ pairs to a tidy table; scoring; Cronbach's alpha
#   STEP 11. GSR: signal check (rule R3)
#   STEP 12. FEA: one column per expression
#   STEP 13. AOI metrics: the Logo hit, NA versus 0, the aggregated file
#   STEP 14. Three time bases: recording start, study start, stimulus onset
#   STEP 15. Sensor Data (P01 to P06): raw samples checked against the metrics exports
#   STEP 16. Data quality and the exclusion rule (R2)
#   STEP 17. Join everything: the long table and the wide table
#   STEP 18. Codebook, cleaning log, QA report, and the output files
#   STEP 19. Figures (PNG, 1400 px wide, 150 dpi)
#   STEP 20. Pipeline counts
#
# How to run (either way works):
#   a) In RStudio: open this file, Session > Set Working Directory > To Source File Location, Source.
#   b) From a terminal, inside the data folder:  Rscript prepare_data.R
# The console output is also written to prepare_output.txt next to this script.
#
# Packages: readr, dplyr, tidyr, purrr, stringr, ggplot2 (tidyverse; Wickham, 2014, explains why the
# result is "tidy": one row per observation, one column per variable) and grid (part of R).
# If a package is missing, run once:
#   install.packages(c("readr", "dplyr", "tidyr", "purrr", "stringr", "ggplot2"))
#
# MODELED layouts (verify on the lab computer before real data go through this script; see
# README.md): the R metrics file names, METADATA lines, and identifier columns; the FEA metric
# columns; the AOI identifier columns and the "Gaze " prefix; the Sensor Data channel names for the
# Aurora and the Shimmer PPG; AOI booleans as "Logo_active" and "Logo_gazed"; the separator of the
# data quality file; "Start (ms)" counted from the start of the study (StartSlide of the first slide).
# =============================================================================

